 1075 #edb/ref 17592186557880 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558467 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186558073 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558467 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186558098 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558467 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186558180 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558467 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558467 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558467 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186558098 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558467 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "e16ab84f-f9e2-51b5-a8b8-31571c55bdac" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558486 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186558365 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558486 #edb/ref 1069 "q-105600" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558486 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558486 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}3 & -7 & 4 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 7 \\\\ -3 & 7 & -6\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & \\boxed{} & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}3 & -7 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -6 & 11 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\n$$\n\nConsider the LU factorization of the matrix $A$ above. What is the value of the entry ${l}_{32}$ in the matrix $L?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558486 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0dbf0280-1ed5-5f59-85a1-40459f1b28eb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558486 #edb/ref 1074 "The matrix $U$ in the LU decomposition is obtained from the original matrix $A$ by row-reducing using Gaussian elementation. In particular, to make zeros below the main diagonal, we proceed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -7 & 4 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 7 \\\\ -3 & 7 & -6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+1{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & -7 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -6 & 11 \\\\ -3 & 7 & -6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+1{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & -7 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -6 & 11 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the position ${a}_{32}$ is already $0.$ Therefore, the entry ${l}_{32}$ of the matrix $L$ is\n\n$$\n{l}_{32}=0.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558487 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186558420 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558487 #edb/ref 1069 "q-107164" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558487 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558487 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ -4 & 0 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n \n\nIf $A=LU$ is the LU decomposition of the matrix $A$ given above, what is the value of ${l}_{43}\\cdot {u}_{34}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558487 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0f1607d8-cc48-5c66-aa9c-59fd264b4966" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558487 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's apply our standard LU factorization algorithm.\n\nWe reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form to obtain the matrix $U,$ as usual. However, instead of writing zeros below the main diagonal, we will write down the entries of the matrix $L.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ -4 & 0 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -2 & 3 \\\\ \\,\\,1\\,\\, & 0 & 2 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 0 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{4} & :={R}_{4}+4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -2 & 3 \\\\ \\,\\,1\\,\\, & 0 & 2 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 0 & -6 & 13\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{4} & :={R}_{4}+3{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -2 & 3 \\\\ \\,\\,1\\,\\, & 0 & 2 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 0 & \\,-3\\, & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\nL=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 0 & -3 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,U=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${l}_{43}=-3$ and ${u}_{34}=-4,$ we get\n\n$$\n{l}_{43}\\cdot {u}_{34}=(-3)\\cdot (-4)=12.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558497 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186558467 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558497 #edb/ref 1069 "q-32810" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558497 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558497 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}5 & 10 & -4 \\\\ -5 & 10 & -2 \\\\ 20 & 40 & -15\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ \\boxed{} & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}5 & 10 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 20 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\n$$\n\nConsider the LU factorization of the matrix $A$ above. What is the value of the entry ${l}_{31}$ in the matrix $L?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558497 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1fff8cfc-a14b-5392-9c5a-4c6f022200cf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558497 #edb/ref 1074 "We want to make $0$ at the position ${a}_{31}$ using Gaussian elimination. To do this, we take the $3$rd row of $A,$ and we add the $1$st row of $A$ multiplied by $-4:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 10 & -4 \\\\ -5 & 10 & -2 \\\\ 20 & 40 & -15\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}5 & 10 & -4 \\\\ -5 & 10 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe entry ${l}_{31}$ of the matrix $L$ is the factor next to ${R}_{1},$ taken with the opposite sign:\n\n$$\n{l}_{31}=-(-4)=4.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558514 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186558465 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558514 #edb/ref 1069 "q-106973" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558514 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558514 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 4 & 14 & -16 \\\\ 3 & 13 & -11\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nIf $A=LU$ is the LU decomposition of the matrix $A$ given above, what is the value of ${l}_{32}\\cdot {u}_{23}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558514 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4e8f6f22-5a79-521e-9e1b-64d5b0f419c0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558514 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's apply our standard LU factorization algorithm.\n\nWe reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form to obtain the matrix $U,$ as usual. However, instead of writing zeros below the main diagonal, we will instead write down the entries of the matrix $L.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 4 & 14 & -16 \\\\ 3 & 13 & -11\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & -4 \\\\ \\,\\,4\\,\\, & 2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 13 & -11\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & -4 \\\\ \\,\\,4\\,\\, & 2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & -4 \\\\ \\,\\,4\\,\\, & 2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & \\,2\\, & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\nL=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,U=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${l}_{32}=2$ and ${u}_{23}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{l}_{32}\\cdot {u}_{23}=2\\cdot 0=0.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558555 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186558462 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558555 #edb/ref 1069 "q-32645" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558555 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558555 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nIf $A=LU$ is the LU decomposition of the matrix $A$ given above, what is the value of ${l}_{31}\\cdot {u}_{23}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558555 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "afd80dcb-1213-5376-8192-e6d4df545870" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558555 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's apply our standard LU factorization algorithm.\n\nWe reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form to obtain the matrix $U,$ as usual. However, instead of writing zeros below the main diagonal, we will instead write down the entries of the matrix $L.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+1{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -3 & 1 \\\\ \\,\\,0\\,\\, & -1 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\nL=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,U=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${l}_{31}=0$ and ${u}_{23}=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n{l}_{31}\\cdot {u}_{23}=0\\cdot 1=0.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558566 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186558400 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558566 #edb/ref 1069 "q-105889" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558566 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558566 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 9 & -3 \\\\ 4 & 12 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 6 & -3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nIf $C=LU$ is the LU decomposition of the matrix $C$ given above, what is the value of ${l}_{21}\\cdot {u}_{23}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558566 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d3f95eef-078c-51a3-be11-2d73ad369bc7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558566 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's apply our standard LU factorization algorithm.\n\nWe reduce the matrix $C$ to row echelon form to obtain the matrix $U,$ as usual. However, instead of writing zeros below the main diagonal, we will instead write down the entries of the matrix $L.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 9 & -3 \\\\ 4 & 12 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 6 & -3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}4 & 9 & -3 \\\\ \\,\\,1\\,\\, & 3 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 6 & -3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}4 & 9 & -3 \\\\ \\,\\,1\\,\\, & 3 & 3 \\\\ \\,\\,1\\,\\, & -3 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+1{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}4 & 9 & -3 \\\\ \\,\\,1\\,\\, & 3 & 3 \\\\ \\,\\,1\\,\\, & -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\nL=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,U=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 9 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${l}_{21}=1$ and ${u}_{23}=3,$ we get\n\n$$\n{l}_{21}\\cdot {u}_{23}=1\\cdot 3=3.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558570 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186558382 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558570 #edb/ref 1069 "q-106962" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558570 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558570 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}7 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 28 & -8 & -3 \\\\ -49 & 37 & 7\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -7 & 4 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}7 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\boxed{}\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}.\n$$\n\nConsider the LU factorization of the matrix $A$ above. What is the value of ${u}_{33}$ in the matrix $U?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558570 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "daed3fa2-ebc7-5729-abaf-5914fd69e7b0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558570 #edb/ref 1074 "The matrix $U$ in the LU decomposition is obtained from the original matrix $A$ by row-reducing using Gaussian elementation. In particular, to make zeros below the main diagonal, we proceed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 28 & -8 & -3 \\\\ -49 & 37 & 7\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}7 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 1 \\\\ -49 & 37 & 7\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+7{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}7 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 16 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-4){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}7 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${u}_{33}=-4.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
